solution:
In an equilateral triangle the altitude $AD$ is also a median and a perpendicular bisector, so the circumcentre $O$ lies on $AD$, and it coincides with the centroid. Hence $AO=\dfrac{2}{3}AD$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(6,0),A=(3,3*sqrt(3)),D=(3,0),O=(3,sqrt(3));
draw(circle(O,2*sqrt(3)),gray+linewidth(0.7));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--D,dashed);
draw(O--C,blue+linewidth(0.8));
draw((3,0)--(3,0.35)--(3.35,0.35)--(3.35,0),linewidth(0.5));
dot(O);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,S);label("$O$",O,W);
label("$6$",(A+B)/2,NW);
label("$R$",(O+C)/2,N,blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Since $D$ is the midpoint of $BC$, $BD=3$ and by Pythagoras $AD=\sqrt{6^2-3^2}=3\sqrt3$. Therefore
\[
R=AO=\dfrac{2}{3}\cdot3\sqrt3=\boxed{2\sqrt3}.
\]
(Check: $OD=\sqrt3$ and $OC=\sqrt{OD^2+DC^2}=\sqrt{3+9}=2\sqrt3$.)

question:
Let $n$ be a positive integer, and let the last $n$ digits of a positive integer $N$ be $a_1a_2\ldots a_n$ (so $a_1$ is the digit in the $10^{n-1}$ place and $a_n$ is the units digit). A student proposes the following test:
\begin{quote}
$N$ is divisible by $2^n$ exactly when $n a_1+(n-1)a_2+(n-2)a_3+\dots+2a_{n-1}+a_n$ is divisible by $2^n$; and the same holds with $5^n$ in place of $2^n$.
\end{quote}
Prove or disprove the test. For which of the cases $2^1,2^2,5^1,5^2,2^3$ is it correct?

solution:
The probability is the area of the region of the rectangle between the circles $x^2+y^2=4$ and $x^2+y^2=15$, divided by the rectangle's area $15$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real r1=2, r2=sqrt(15);
pair O=(0,0), Q=(sqrt(6),3);
real th=degrees(atan(3/sqrt(6)));
path reg=arc(O,r2,0,th)--(0,3)--(0,r1)--arc(O,r1,90,0)--cycle;
fill(reg,lightgray);
draw((0,0)--(5,0)--(5,3)--(0,3)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(arc(O,r1,0,90),linewidth(0.8));
draw(arc(O,r2,0,th),linewidth(0.8));
draw(O--Q,dashed);
dot(Q);
label("$O$",O,SW);label("$(5,0)$",(5,0),S);label("$(5,3)$",(5,3),NE);label("$(0,3)$",(0,3),W);
label("$Q(\sqrt6,3)$",Q,N);
label("$2$",(2,0),S);label("$\sqrt{15}$",(sqrt(15),0),S);
draw(arc(O,0.75,0,th),linewidth(0.5));
label("$\theta$",1.05*dir(th/2));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Inside the larger circle.} Since $\sqrt{15}<5$, the circle of radius $\sqrt{15}$ does not reach the side $x=5$, but it crosses the top side $y=3$ at $Q=(\sqrt6,3)$, because $6+9=15$. The part of the rectangle inside this circle is the right triangle with vertices $O$, $(0,3)$, $Q$, of area $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot3\cdot\sqrt6=\dfrac{3\sqrt6}{2}$, together with the circular sector between the $x$-axis and $OQ$, of angle $\theta=\arctan\dfrac{3}{\sqrt6}=\arctan\dfrac{\sqrt6}{2}$ and area $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot15\,\theta$.

\textbf{Inside the smaller circle.} The quarter disc of radius $2$ lies inside the rectangle (as $2<3$) and has area $\dfrac{1}{4}\cdot4\pi=\pi$.

So the shaded region has area $\dfrac{3\sqrt6}{2}+\dfrac{15}{2}\arctan\dfrac{\sqrt6}{2}-\pi$, and the probability is
\[
\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt6}{10}+\dfrac{1}{2}\arctan\dfrac{\sqrt6}{2}-\dfrac{\pi}{15}}\approx0.479 .
\]

Triangle $ADC$ has a right angle at $D$, so
\[
AD^2+DC^2=13^2=169,\qquad \dfrac{1}{2}\,AD\cdot DC=30\ \Longrightarrow\ AD\cdot DC=60 .
\]
Then $(AD+DC)^2=169+120=289$ and $(AD-DC)^2=169-120=49$, so $AD+DC=17$, $|AD-DC|=7$, and $\{AD,DC\}=\{12,5\}$.

\textbf{Case $AD=12$, $DC=5$.} In right triangle $ADB$, $BD=\sqrt{15^2-12^2}=9$. In an acute triangle the foot of an altitude lies between the other two vertices, so $BC=BD+DC=14$ and
\[
[ABC]=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot14\cdot12=84 .
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(14,0),A=(9,12),D=(9,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--D,linewidth(0.8));
draw((9,0)--(9,0.7)--(8.3,0.7)--(8.3,0),linewidth(0.5));
fill(A--D--C--cycle,lightgray+opacity(0.5));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));draw(A--D,linewidth(0.8));
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,S);
label("$15$",(A+B)/2,NW);label("$13$",(A+C)/2,NE);
label("$12$",(9,6),W);
label("$9$",(4.5,0),S);label("$5$",(11.5,0),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Case $AD=5$, $DC=12$.} Then $BD=\sqrt{225-25}=10\sqrt2$, and the area $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot5\,(10\sqrt2+12)=30+25\sqrt2$ is not an integer. (This triangle, with $BC=10\sqrt2+12$, is not even acute: $15^2+13^2=394<BC^2$.)

Hence the area of triangle $ABC$ is $\boxed{84}$; it is the $13$-$14$-$15$ triangle.

Let $n$ have a proper divisor, and let $p$ be its smallest prime factor. Then the smallest proper divisor is $p$ and the largest is $\dfrac {n}{p}$. The condition $\dfrac {n}{p}=45p$ gives $n=45p^2=3^2\cdot5\cdot p^2$.

For $p$ to be the smallest prime factor of $n$ we need $p\le3$, since $3\mid n$. Both values work:
\begin{itemize}
\item $p=2$: $n=180$, smallest proper divisor $2$, largest $90=45\cdot2$;
\item $p=3$: $n=405$, smallest proper divisor $3$, largest $135=45\cdot3$.
\end{itemize}
So there are exactly two such numbers: $\boxed{\textbf{(C)}\ 2}$.

\textbf{$A$.} With $y=\dfrac{1}{3}$: $3x+3=2$, so $x=-\dfrac{1}{3}$ and $A=\left(-\dfrac{1}{3},\dfrac{1}{3}\right)$.

\textbf{$B$.} With $x=\dfrac{2}{3}$: $2+9y=2$, so $y=0$ and $B=\left(\dfrac{2}{3},0\right)$.

\textbf{Slope.} Through $A$ and $B$,
\[
m=\dfrac{0-\dfrac{1}{3}}{\dfrac{2}{3}-\left(-\dfrac{1}{3}\right)}=\dfrac{-\dfrac{1}{3}}{1}=\boxed{-\dfrac{1}{3}}.
\]
(Equivalently, $y=-\dfrac{1}{3}x+\dfrac{2}{9}$.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real f(real x){return (2-3x)/9;}
draw((-1.7,0)--(1.4,0),Arrow(6));draw((0,-0.45)--(0,0.8),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(1.4,0),E);label("$y$",(0,0.8),N);
draw(graph(f,-1.6,1.3),blue+linewidth(0.9));
pair A=(-1/3,1/3),B=(2/3,0);
dot(A);dot(B);
draw((-1/3,0)--A--(0,1/3),dashed+gray);
label("$A$",A,2*dir(215));label("$-\dfrac{1}{3}$",(-1/3,0),S);draw((0,1/3)--(0.04,1/3));label("$\dfrac{1}{3}$",(0.03,1/3),dir(20),fontsize(9pt));
label("$B$",B,SW);label("$\dfrac{2}{3}$",(2/3,0),2*N);
label("$3x+9y=2$",(1.3,f(1.3)),SE,blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

A circle of area $\pi r^2$ has radius $r$, so the two circles have radii $4$ (since $\pi\cdot4^2=16\pi$) and $10$ (since $\pi\cdot10^2=100\pi$). Their average is $\dfrac{4+10}{2}=7$, and the circle of radius $7$ has area $\pi\cdot7^2=\boxed{49\pi}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);
draw(circle(O,10),linewidth(0.8));
draw(circle(O,7),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(O,4),linewidth(0.8));
dot(O);
draw(O--4*dir(200),linewidth(0.6));draw(O--7*dir(90),blue+linewidth(0.6));draw(O--10*dir(-30),linewidth(0.6));
label("$4$",2*dir(200),dir(110));
label("$7$",(0,4.6),E,blue);
label("$10$",8.5*dir(-30),dir(60));
label("$O$",O,dir(-115));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

(Note that this is not the average of the two areas, $58\pi$.)

question:
Find the value of $b$ if the graph of $y=-3x+b$ passes through the given point.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $A(-2,4)$
\item[(b)] $B(5,2)$
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Triangle $AOP$ has a right angle at $O$, with $AO=2$ and $OP=\dfrac{3}{2}$, so
\[
AP=\sqrt{4+\dfrac{9}{4}}=\dfrac{5}{2} .
\]
Since $AB$ is a diameter, $\angle ACB=90^\circ$. Triangles $AOP$ and $ACB$ are right-angled (at $O$ and at $C$) and share the angle at $A$, so they are similar, and
\[
\dfrac{AC}{AB}=\dfrac{AO}{AP}\ \Longrightarrow\ AC=4\cdot\dfrac{2}{\dfrac{5}{2}}=\boxed{\dfrac{16}{5}}.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0),A=(-2,0),B=(2,0),P=(0,1.5),C=(14/25,48/25);
draw(circle(O,2),linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--B,linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--C,linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--P,linewidth(0.8));
draw(C--B,dashed);
draw((0,0)--(0.22,0)--(0.22,0.22)--(0,0.22),linewidth(0.5));
pair u=unit(A-C),v=unit(B-C);
draw(C+0.22*u--C+0.22*(u+v)--C+0.22*v,linewidth(0.5)+gray);
dot(O);dot(P);
label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,E);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$O$",O,S);label("$P$",P,NW);
label("$\dfrac{3}{2}$",(0,0.75),E);
label("$2$",(-1,0),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

(Check with coordinates: $O=(0,0)$, $A=(-2,0)$, $P=\left(0,\dfrac{3}{2}\right)$; line $AP$ meets the circle again at $C=\left(\dfrac{14}{25},\dfrac{48}{25}\right)$, and $AC=\sqrt{\left(\dfrac{64}{25}\right)^2+\left(\dfrac{48}{25}\right)^2}=\dfrac{80}{25}=\dfrac{16}{5}$.)

Right triangles $ABX$ and $ADY$ have $AB=AD$ and $AX=AY$, so they are congruent (hypotenuse-leg) and $BX=DY=t$, say. Then $CX=CY=1-t$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real t=sqrt(2)/2;
pair A=(0,0),B=(1,0),C=(1,1),D=(0,1),X=(1,t),Y=(t,1);
fill(A--X--Y--cycle,lightgray);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--X--Y--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);
label("$X$",X,E);label("$Y$",Y,NW);
label("$t$",(1,t/2),E);label("$t$",(t/2,1),N);
label("$1-t$",(1,(1+t)/2),E,fontsize(8pt));label("$1-t$",((1+t)/2,1),N,fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The triangle $AXY$ is the square minus three right triangles:
\[
[AXY]=1-\dfrac {t}{2}-\dfrac {t}{2}-\dfrac{(1-t)^2}{2}.
\]
With $u=1-t$ this is $u-\dfrac{u^2}{2}$, and setting it equal to $\dfrac{1}{4}$ gives $2u^2-4u+1=0$, so $u=1\pm\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}$. Since $0\le u\le1$, $u=1-\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}$. Finally $XY$ is the hypotenuse of the isosceles right triangle $XCY$:
\[
XY=u\sqrt2=\sqrt2-1 .
\]
So $XY=\boxed{\sqrt2-1}$. (Check: $t=\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}$ and $[AXY]=1-\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}-\dfrac{\left(1-\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}\right)^2}{2}=\dfrac{1}{4}$.)

question:
Let $D_n$ be the number of derangements of $n$ objects (arrangements in which no object is in its original place), with $D_1=0$ and $D_2=1$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $D_{n+2}=(n+1)\left(D_{n+1}+D_n\right)$ for every positive integer $n$. So in the recurrence $D_{n+2}=aD_{n+1}+bD_n$ given by this argument, $a=b=n+1$; what is $\dfrac {b}{a}$?
\item[(b)] Show that there are no constants $a,b$ (independent of $n$) with $D_{n+2}=aD_{n+1}+bD_n$ for all $n$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the square be $ABCD$ (clockwise) and the point $P$, with $PA=1$, $PB=5$, $PC=7$, $PD=5$.

Since $PB=PD$, $P$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of the diagonal $BD$, which is the other diagonal $AC$. As $P$ is inside the square, it lies on segment $AC$, so
\[
AC=PA+PC=1+7=8 .
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=4*sqrt(2);
pair A=(0,0),B=(0,s),C=(s,s),D=(s,0),P=(1/sqrt(2),1/sqrt(2));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--C,dashed+gray);
draw(P--A,blue+linewidth(0.8));draw(P--B,blue+linewidth(0.8));draw(P--C,blue+linewidth(0.8));draw(P--D,blue+linewidth(0.8));
dot(P);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,NW);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,SE);label("$P$",P,2*dir(16));
label("$1$",(P+A)/2,NW,fontsize(9pt));
label("$5$",(P+B)/2,E);label("$7$",(P+C)/2+(0.15,-0.15),SE);label("$5$",(P+D)/2,N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

A square with diagonal $8$ has area $\dfrac{8^2}{2}=\boxed{32}$.

Check: the side is $4\sqrt2$, and with $A=(0,0)$, $B=(0,4\sqrt2)$, $P=\left(\dfrac{1}{\sqrt2},\dfrac{1}{\sqrt2}\right)$ one gets $PB^2=\dfrac{1}{2}+\dfrac{49}{2}=25$, as required.

Work in centimetres: the bar is a cylinder of radius $2$ cm and length $729$ cm.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real L=6, rr=0.5;
draw((0,rr)--(L,rr),linewidth(0.8));draw((0,-rr)--(L,-rr),linewidth(0.8));
draw(ellipse((0,0),0.2,rr),linewidth(0.8));
draw(shift((L,0))*scale(0.2,rr)*arc((0,0),1,-90,90),linewidth(0.8));
draw(shift((L,0))*scale(0.2,rr)*arc((0,0),1,90,270),dashed+gray);
draw((0,-rr-0.35)--(L,-rr-0.35),Arrows(5));
label("$729$ cm",(L/2,-rr-0.35),S);
draw((-0.45,-rr)--(-0.45,rr),Arrows(5));
label("$4$ cm",(-0.45,0),W);
pair Cs=(L+2.3,0);
draw(circle(Cs,1.2),linewidth(0.8));
draw(ellipse(Cs,1.2,0.3),gray);
dot(Cs);
draw(Cs--Cs+1.2*dir(-40),linewidth(0.6));
label("$9$ cm",Cs+0.55*dir(-40),SW,fontsize(9pt));
label("(not to scale)",(L/2,rr+0.25),N,fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\[
V_{\text{bar}}=\pi\cdot2^2\cdot729=2916\pi\ \text{cm}^3,\qquad V_{\text{ball}}=\dfrac{4}{3}\pi\cdot9^3=972\pi\ \text{cm}^3 .
\]
Since $\dfrac{2916\pi}{972\pi}=3$ exactly, the metal makes $\boxed{\textbf{(a)}\ 3}$ balls.

The points are the vertices of a regular hexagon, and any $3$ of them form a triangle, so there are $\dbinom63=20$ equally likely choices.

Up to rotation and reflection there are three kinds of triangle:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);
pair[] V; string[] nm={"$A$","$B$","$C$","$D$","$E$","$F$"};
for(int i=0;i<6;++i) V.push(dir(90-60*i));
draw(circle(O,1),gray+linewidth(0.6));
draw(V[0]--V[2]--V[4]--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[5]--cycle,red+dashed+linewidth(0.9));
for(int i=0;i<6;++i){dot(V[i]);label(nm[i],V[i],dir(90-60*i));}
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\begin{itemize}
\item three consecutive vertices, such as $FAB$ (dashed red): sides $s,s,s\sqrt3$, isosceles; there are $6$ of these (one for each middle vertex);
\item alternate vertices $ACE$ or $BDF$ (blue): equilateral, hence isosceles; there are $2$;
\item the remaining $20-6-2=12$ triangles, such as $ABD$, contain a diameter: sides $s$, $s\sqrt3$, $2s$, all different (a $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle).
\end{itemize}
So $8$ of the $20$ triangles are isosceles, and the probability is $\dfrac{8}{20}=\boxed{\dfrac{2}{5}}$.

By clue 5 every digit is one of $0,1,4,6,8,9$. By clue 6 the first two digits add to $15$, and the only such pairs from these digits are $6,9$ and $9,6$ (the pair $7,8$ uses the prime $7$). So the number starts with $69$ or $96$.

By clue 3 it is below $500$, so it cannot have three digits starting with $6$ or $9$; it is $69$ or $96$. Clue 1 rules out $96$, since $\dfrac{96}{3}=32$ is not prime. The number $69$ satisfies every clue:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\dfrac{69}{3}=23$ is prime;
\item $2\cdot69=138$ and $1+3+8=12$;
\item $50<69<500$;
\item $\dfrac{6}{9}=\dfrac{2}{3}$ and $\dfrac{10}{6+9}=\dfrac{2}{3}$;
\item $6$ and $9$ are not prime;
\item $6+9=15$;
\item the divisors of $69$ are $1,3,23,69$.
\end{enumerate}
The number is $\boxed{69}$.

A regular dodecagon has $12$ equal central angles (each subtended by one side at the centre), and together they make a full turn. So each measures $\dfrac{360^\circ}{12}=\boxed{30^\circ}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
