In the same way $c+a=\dfrac{K}{55}$ and $a+b=\dfrac{K}{60}$. Adding,
\[
2(a+b+c)=K\left(\dfrac{1}{66}+\dfrac{1}{55}+\dfrac{1}{60}\right)=K\cdot\dfrac{10+12+11}{660}=\dfrac{K}{20}.
\]
The inradius $r$ satisfies $K=r(a+b+c)$, so
\[
