question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many distinct $7$-digit numbers can be made by rearranging the digits of $2335142$?
\item[(b)] How many distinct $5$-digit numbers can be made using five of these seven digits (each digit used at most as often as it occurs in $2335142$)?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The largest sphere is the inscribed sphere, tangent to all four faces; its centre $I$ is the centre of the tetrahedron. Joining $I$ to the four vertices splits the tetrahedron into four pyramids, each with a face as base and height $r$. So
\[
V=\dfrac{1}{3}\,r\cdot(\text{total surface area}).
\]
For edge $1$: each face has area $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}$, so the surface area is $\sqrt3$. The height of the tetrahedron is $DG$, where $G$ is the centroid of the base $ABC$ and $AG=\dfrac{2}{3}\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{3}$, so $DG=\sqrt{1-\dfrac{1}{3}}=\sqrt{\dfrac{2}{3}}=\dfrac{\sqrt6}{3}$. The volume is $V=\dfrac{1}{3}\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt6}{3}=\dfrac{\sqrt2}{12}$. Hence
\[
r=\dfrac{3V}{\sqrt3}=\dfrac{\sqrt2}{4\sqrt3}=\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt6}{12}}.
\]
The figure shows the cross-section through the apex $D$, the vertex $A$ and the midpoint $M$ of $BC$; the sphere meets it in a circle tangent to $AM$ (in the base) and to $DM$ (in the face $DBC$, which is perpendicular to this plane).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real h=sqrt(2/3);
pair A=(0,0),M=(sqrt(3)/2,0),D=(sqrt(3)/3,h),G=(sqrt(3)/3,0);
real r=h/4;pair I=(sqrt(3)/3,r);
draw(A--M--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(D--G,dashed);
draw(circle(I,r),blue);
pair T=M+dot(I-M,unit(D-M))*unit(D-M);
draw(I--T,blue+dashed);dot(I);dot(G);dot(T);
draw((G+(0.03,0))--(G+(0.03,0.03))--(G+(0,0.03)));
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$M$",M,SE);label("$D$",D,N);label("$G$",G,S);label("$I$",I,W);label("$r$",(I+T)/2,NE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Equivalently, $r=\dfrac{1}{4}DG$: the centre divides each height in the ratio $3:1$.

Each pair of opposite sides of the parallelogram lies along the two edges of one ribbon, so the distance between those sides is that ribbon's width.

Take as base a side lying along an edge of the $4$ mm ribbon; the corresponding height is $4$, so this side has length $\dfrac{60}{4}=15$. Take as base a side along an edge of the $12$ mm ribbon; the height is $12$, so this side has length $\dfrac{60}{12}=5$.

\begin{asy}
