label("width $4$",(-3,2));label("width $12$",(-2.25,-3)+10.5/0.8*u+(6,0.6),N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The perimeter is $2(15+5)=\boxed{40}$ mm. (The ribbons cross at an angle $\theta$ with $\sin\theta=\dfrac{60}{15\cdot5}=\dfrac{4}{5}$, which is possible; the figure is drawn with this angle.)

Each of the $2^{12}=4096$ outcomes (which coins show heads) is equally likely. Let $p$, $n$, $d$ be the numbers of pennies, nickels and dimes showing heads; we need $p+5n+10d=30$. Since $p\le5$ and $p$ must be a multiple of $5$, $p=0$ or $p=5$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $p=0$: $5n+10d=30$, i.e.\ $n+2d=6$: $(n,d)=(0,3),(2,2),(4,1)$, giving $\dbinom40\dbinom33+\dbinom42\dbinom32+\dbinom44\dbinom31=1+18+3=22$ outcomes.
\item $p=5$: $n+2d=5$: $(n,d)=(1,2),(3,1)$ ($n=5$ is impossible), giving $\dbinom41\dbinom32+\dbinom43\dbinom31=12+12=24$ outcomes.
\end{itemize}
In total $22+24=46$ outcomes, and the probability is $\dfrac{46}{4096}=\boxed{\dfrac{23}{2048}}$.

The cone has height $\sqrt{5^2-3^2}=4$. Its lateral area is $\pi\cdot3\cdot5=15\pi$ and its base area is $9\pi$. Let the small cone (with the same apex $V$) be similar to the whole cone with ratio $k$, $0<k<1$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
