draw((3.8,0)--(3.8,yc),Arrows(4));draw((3.1,0)--(4,0),dotted);draw((3k+0.2,yc)--(4,yc),dotted);
label("$d$",(3.8,yc/2),E);label("$V$",V,N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The small cone has radius $3k$ and slant height $5k$, so its total surface area is $15\pi k^2+9\pi k^2=24\pi k^2$. The frustum has lateral area $15\pi-15\pi k^2$, bottom $9\pi$ and top $9\pi k^2$, in total $24\pi-6\pi k^2$. The condition is
\[
\dfrac{24k^2}{24-6k^2}=\dfrac{1}{5}\ \Longrightarrow\ 120k^2=24-6k^2\ \Longrightarrow\ k^2=\dfrac{4}{21},\quad k=\dfrac{2}{\sqrt{21}} .
\]
(The reverse ratio $5:1$ would need $k^2=\dfrac{20}{9}>1$, which is impossible, so the configuration is unique.) The plane is at height $4k$ below the apex, so its distance to the base is
\[
d=4-4k=4-\dfrac{8}{\sqrt{21}}=\boxed{4-\dfrac{8\sqrt{21}}{21}}\approx2.25 .
\]

Let $n=2019$, let $R$ be the circumradius and $r$ the inradius (apothem) of the polygon. The figure shows a regular $12$-gon with the same notation; $O$ is the centre and the dashed circle is the incircle.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
int n=12; real R=1; real r=R*cos(pi/n);
pair O=(0,0);
pair[] P; for(int k=0;k<n;++k) P.push(R*dir(90+360*k/n));
path g=P[0]; for(int k=1;k<n;++k) g=g--P[k]; g=g--cycle;
draw(g,linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(O,r),dashed+gray);
pair Mid=(P[6]+P[7])/2;
draw(O--Mid,blue+linewidth(0.7)); draw(O--P[7],dashed);
dot(O); label("$O$",O,N);
label("$r$",(O+Mid)/2,dir(degrees(Mid-O)-90),blue);
label("$R$",(O+P[7])/2,dir(degrees(P[7]-O)+90));
pair u=unit(P[7]-P[6]); pair v=(-u.y,u.x);
draw(Mid+0.05*u--Mid+0.05*u+0.05*v--Mid+0.05*v);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Cutting the polygon into $n$ triangles with apex $O$, each with base a side and height $r$, gives
\[
\text{Area}=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot(\text{perimeter})\cdot r=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot2\pi\cdot r=\pi r .
\]
\textbf{Upper bound.} The polygon contains its incircle, and its perimeter is longer than the circumference of that circle, so $2\pi>2\pi r$, i.e.\ $r<1$.

\textbf{Lower bound.} The polygon is inscribed in its circumcircle, so its perimeter is shorter than that circumference: $2\pi<2\pi R$, i.e.\ $R>1$. Also $r=R\cos\dfrac{\pi}{n}$ and $\dfrac{\pi} {n}<0.01$, so $r>\cos0.01>1-\dfrac{0.01^2}{2}>0.99$.

Hence $3.11<0.99\pi<\text{Area}<\pi<3.15$, and the area to the nearest integer is $\boxed{3}$. (Exactly, the area is $\dfrac{\pi^2}{n}\cot\dfrac{\pi}{n}\approx3.14159$, extremely close to the area $\pi$ of a circle of perimeter $2\pi$.)

question:
In the diagram, $O$ is the centre of the circle and $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$ lie on the circle. Chords $AB$ and $CD$ are parallel, $\angle ABO=24^\circ$, and $\angle OBC=\angle OCD$. Find $\angle BOC$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);
pair A=dir(260),B=dir(128),C=dir(52),D=dir(-24);
draw(unitcircle,linewidth(0.7));
draw(A--B--C--D,linewidth(0.9));
draw(B--O--C,linewidth(0.7));
dot(O);dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);
label("$O$",O,S);label("$A$",A,dir(260));label("$B$",B,dir(128));label("$C$",C,dir(52));label("$D$",D,dir(-24));
draw(arc(B,0.45,-76,-52),linewidth(0.6));
label("$24^\circ$",B+0.74*dir(-64));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

solution:
Let $\angle OBC=\angle OCD=x$. Since $OB=OC$ (radii), triangle $OBC$ is isosceles and $\angle OCB=\angle OBC=x$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);
pair A=dir(260),B=dir(128),C=dir(52),D=dir(-24);
draw(unitcircle,linewidth(0.7));
draw(A--B--C--D,linewidth(0.9));
draw(B--O--C,linewidth(0.7));
dot(O);dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);
label("$O$",O,S);label("$A$",A,dir(260));label("$B$",B,dir(128));label("$C$",C,dir(52));label("$D$",D,dir(-24));
draw(arc(B,0.45,-76,-52),linewidth(0.6));
label("$24^\circ$",B+0.74*dir(-64));
draw(arc(B,0.3,-52,0),blue+linewidth(0.6)); label("$x$",B+0.4*dir(-26),blue);
draw(arc(C,0.3,180,232),blue+linewidth(0.6)); label("$x$",C+0.4*dir(206),blue);
draw(arc(C,0.22,232,284),blue+linewidth(0.6)); label("$x$",C+0.32*dir(258),blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

As in the figure, $O$ lies inside the angles $ABC$ and $BCD$, so
\[
\angle ABC=\angle ABO+\angle OBC=24^\circ+x,\qquad \angle BCD=\angle BCO+\angle OCD=2x .
\]
Because $AB\parallel CD$ and $A$, $D$ are on the same side of the transversal $BC$, these co-interior angles add up to $180^\circ$:
\[
24^\circ+x+2x=180^\circ\ \Longrightarrow\ x=52^\circ .
\]
Finally, in triangle $OBC$, $\angle BOC=180^\circ-2x=180^\circ-104^\circ=\boxed{76^\circ}$.

question:
A student writes that the number of ways to put $n$ indistinguishable objects into $k$ indistinguishable boxes is $\dbinom{n+k-1}{k}$, and asks whether this requires every box to get at least one object. Correct the statement, and illustrate with $n=7$ objects and $k=3$ boxes: find the number of ways when
\begin{enumerate}
\item the boxes are distinguishable and boxes may be empty;
\item the boxes are distinguishable and every box gets at least one object;
\item the boxes are indistinguishable and boxes may be empty.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Stars and bars counts placements of $n$ identical objects into $k$ \emph{distinguishable} boxes. A placement is a row of $n$ stars and $k-1$ bars (the bars separate the boxes), so there are $\dbinom{n+k-1}{k-1}$ of them, and \emph{empty boxes are allowed} (two adjacent bars). If every box must get at least one object, first put one object in each box and distribute the remaining $n-k$ freely: $\dbinom{n-1}{k-1}$ ways. For indistinguishable boxes there is no such binomial formula: one counts partitions of $n$ into at most $k$ parts.

For $n=7$, $k=3$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\dbinom{9}{2}=\boxed{36}$;
\item $\dbinom{6}{2}=\boxed{15}$;
\item the partitions of $7$ into at most $3$ parts: $7,\ 6+1,\ 5+2,\ 4+3,\ 5+1+1,\ 4+2+1,\ 3+3+1,\ 3+2+2$, so $\boxed{8}$.
\end{enumerate}

Draw the lines through $P$ parallel to the sides (dashed). Let $r$ and $s$ be the distances from $P$ to $AD$ and to $BC$, and $p$ and $t$ the distances from $P$ to $AB$ and to $CD$; in the figure they appear as the pieces of the sides $AB$ and $AD$.
