Triangle $PHG$ has a right angle at $H$, so by the Pythagorean theorem
\[
x^2+9^2=(27-x)^2\ \Longrightarrow\ x^2+81=729-54x+x^2\ \Longrightarrow\ 54x=648\ \Longrightarrow\ x=12 .
\]
The eagle grabs the snake $\boxed{12}$ ft from the bottom of the pillar. Check: the eagle flies $\sqrt{144+81}=15$ ft and the snake slides $27-12=15$ ft.

The centres of the base spheres are at height $r$ and form a square of side $2r$ (neighbouring spheres are tangent). Its centre $O$ is at distance $\dfrac{2r\sqrt2}{2}=r\sqrt2$ from each corner. The centre of the top sphere lies above $O$ at distance $2r$ from each base centre. The figure shows the vertical cross-section through two opposite base spheres (left) and the triangle formed by their centres and the top centre (right).

