Here $V-114+56=2$, so $V=\boxed{60}$.

The semiperimeter is $s=\dfrac{5+9+12}{2}=13$. By Heron's formula the area is
\[
K=\sqrt{13(13-5)(13-9)(13-12)}=\sqrt{13\cdot8\cdot4\cdot1}=\sqrt{416}=4\sqrt{26}.
\]
The area also equals $rs$ (split the triangle into three triangles with apex at the incentre $I$ and height $r$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(12,0),A=(11/3,2*sqrt(26)/3);
real a=12,bb=9,c=5;
pair I=(a*A+bb*B+c*C)/(a+bb+c);
real r=4*sqrt(26)/13;
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(I,r),blue);
pair F=(I.x,0);
draw(I--F,red);dot(I);dot(F,red);
draw(F+(0.3,0)--F+(0.3,0.3)--F+(0,0.3),red);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$I$",I,NE);
label("$r$",(I+F)/2,W,red);
label("$12$",(B+C)/2+(2.5,0),S);label("$5$",(A+B)/2,NW);label("$9$",(A+C)/2,NE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

So
\[
r=\dfrac {K}{s}=\dfrac{4\sqrt{26}}{13}\approx\boxed{1.57}\text{ inches}.
\]

Let the Fibonacci numbers be $F_1=F_2=1$, $F_{m+1}=F_m+F_{m-1}$. The sequence is non-decreasing, and strictly increasing from $F_2$ on. Let the three distinct values be $a<b<c$. Write $b=F_k$ and $c=F_l$, choosing the indices so that $k\ge2$; since $c>b$ we have $l\ge k+1$. As $a<b=F_k$ and $a$ is a Fibonacci number, $a\le F_{k-1}$ (with $k\ge 3$, because $a<b$ forces $b\ge2$). Hence
\[
a+b\le F_{k-1}+F_k=F_{k+1}\le F_l=c .
\]
A triangle needs the sum of the two shorter sides to be \emph{greater} than the longest side, so $a,b,c$ are never the sides of a triangle: at best $a+b=c$ and the ``triangle'' is flat.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(8,0);
draw(A--B,linewidth(1));
draw(arc(A,3,0,75),blue+dashed);draw(arc(B,5,105,180),red+dashed);
draw(A--3*dir(55),blue);draw(B--B+5*dir(150),red);
dot(A);dot(B);dot((3,0));
label("$F_j$",1.5*dir(55),NW,blue);label("$F_k$",B+2.5*dir(150),NE,red);
label("$F_{k+1}=F_j+F_k$",(4,0),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The sketch shows the extreme case $a=F_j$, $b=F_k$ with $j=k-1$ and $c=F_{k+1}$ (here $3,5,8$): the two shorter segments, swung from the ends of the longest one, can meet only on it, so they do not form a triangle. $\blacksquare$

Three segments $a<b<c$ form a triangle exactly when $a+b>c$ (the triangle inequality for the longest side). Each length here is the sum of the two before it, so for any choice $a<b<c$ from the list,
\[
a+b\le(\text{the length just before }b)+b=(\text{the length just after }b)\le c .
\]
For example $5+8=13$, $3+5=8$, $3+8<13$. So no choice works:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(13,0);
draw(A--B,linewidth(1));
draw(arc(A,5,0,80),blue+dashed);draw(arc(B,8,100,180),red+dashed);
draw(A--5*dir(60),blue);draw(B--B+8*dir(150),red);
dot(A);dot(B);
label("$5$",2.5*dir(60),NW,blue);label("$8$",B+4*dir(150),NE,red);
label("$13$",(6.5,0),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

the segments of lengths $5$ and $8$ swung from the ends of the segment of length $13$ can only meet on it. The number of triangles is $\boxed{0}$.

