\end{asy}

As the angle from the hour hand to the minute hand goes once round, from $0^\circ$ to $360^\circ$, $d$ increases from $1$ to $7$ (at $180^\circ$) and decreases back to $1$. So in each full turn the integer values $2,3,4,5,6$ occur twice each and $1$ and $7$ once each: $12$ times.

The minute hand gains $330^\circ$ per hour on the hour hand, so in the $12$ hours it gains $12\cdot330^\circ=11\cdot360^\circ$: eleven full turns of the relative angle. At $1{:}00$ (start and end) the angle is $30^\circ$ and $d^2=25-12\sqrt3$ is not an integer, so no occurrence is double-counted at the endpoints. The answer is $11\cdot12=\boxed{132}$.

Place $A=(0,0)$, $B=(1,0)$, $C=(1,1)$, $D=(0,1)$ and $P=(t,1)$ with $0\le t\le1$. By the angle bisector theorem in triangle $APB$,
\[
\dfrac{AQ}{QB}=\dfrac{PA}{PB},\qquad\text{so}\qquad AQ=\dfrac{PA}{PA+PB}=\dfrac{1}{1+\dfrac{PB}{PA}},
\]
where $PA=\sqrt{t^2+1}$ and $PB=\sqrt{(1-t)^2+1}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(1,0),C=(1,1),D=(0,1);
real t=0.3; pair P=(t,1);
real pa=abs(P-A), pb=abs(P-B); pair Q=(pa/(pa+pb),0);
pair Q0=(sqrt(2)-1,0), Q1=(2-sqrt(2),0);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(P--A,linewidth(0.7));draw(P--B,linewidth(0.7));
draw(P--Q,blue+linewidth(0.8));
draw(Q0--Q1,red+linewidth(2.2));
real a1=degrees(A-P),a2=degrees(Q-P),a3=degrees(B-P);
draw(arc(P,0.14,a1,a2),linewidth(0.5));draw(arc(P,0.14,a2,a3),linewidth(0.5));
draw((P+0.11*dir((a1+a2)/2))--(P+0.17*dir((a1+a2)/2)),linewidth(0.5));
draw((P+0.11*dir((a2+a3)/2))--(P+0.17*dir((a2+a3)/2)),linewidth(0.5));
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);
label("$P$",P,N);label("$Q$",Q,NE);
label("$Q_0$",Q0,S,red);label("$Q_1$",Q1,S,red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

As $t$ increases from $0$ to $1$, $PA$ increases and $PB$ decreases, so $\dfrac{PB}{PA}$ decreases and $AQ$ increases strictly and continuously. Hence $Q$ sweeps out the segment from $Q_0$ (for $P=D$) to $Q_1$ (for $P=C$), shown in red:
\[
AQ_0=\dfrac{1}{1+\sqrt2}=\sqrt2-1,\qquad AQ_1=\dfrac{\sqrt2}{\sqrt2+1}=2-\sqrt2 .
\]
The length of the segment is $(2-\sqrt2)-(\sqrt2-1)=\boxed{3-2\sqrt2}$.

Let the pizza have radius $R$ and centre $O$. The largest circle inside a $45^\circ$ sector is tangent to both straight edges and to the arc, so its centre $K$ lies on the bisector of the sector, at distance $R-r$ from $O$, where $r$ is its radius.

