question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain why there is no triangle with side lengths $3$, $13$ and $16$.
\item[(b)] The triangle with side lengths $\sqrt{41}$, $13$ and $16$ has area $40$. Find the length of the altitude to its longest side.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
(a) Since $3+13=16$, the triangle inequality fails (the "triangle" would be a flat segment of area $0$), so no triangle has these side lengths, and certainly none of area $40$.

(b) The longest side is $16$ (as $\sqrt{41}<7<13$). If $h$ is the altitude to it, then $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot16\cdot h=40$, so $h=\boxed{5}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(16,0),A=(12,5),H=(12,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--H,dashed+blue);
draw((H+(-0.5,0))--(H+(-0.5,0.5))--(H+(0,0.5)),linewidth(0.4));
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$H$",H,S);
label("$13$",(A+B)/2,NW);label("$\sqrt{41}$",(A+C)/2,NE);label("$16$",(B+C)/2,S);
label("$h$",(A+H)/2,W,blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Check: put $B=(0,0)$, $C=(16,0)$, $A=(12,5)$. Then $AB=\sqrt{144+25}=13$, $AC=\sqrt{16+25}=\sqrt{41}$, and the area is $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot16\cdot5=40$.

The area of the triangle is $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot6\cdot8=24$. The line through the origin has $y$-intercept $0$; write it as $y=mx$ with $m>0$ (for $m\le0$ the line has $y\le0$ whenever $x\ge0$, so it misses the interior of the triangle, and the line $x=0$ touches it only at $A$). It crosses $AB$ (the line $4x+y=8$) at $P$ and $AC$ (the line $x+y=8$) at $Q$, cutting off triangle $APQ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0),A=(0,8),B=(2,0),C=(8,0);
real m=(-5+sqrt(41))/2;
pair P=(8/(4+m),8*m/(4+m)), Q=(8/(1+m),8*m/(1+m));
fill(A--P--Q--cycle,rgb(0.8,0.88,1));
draw((-0.5,0)--(10,0),Arrow(4));draw((0,-0.5)--(0,9),Arrow(4));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--(9.2,9.2*m),red+linewidth(0.8));
dot(P);dot(Q);
label("$A(0,8)$",A,W);label("$B(2,0)$",B,S);label("$C(8,0)$",C,S);label("$O$",O,SW);
label("$P$",P,1.6*dir(165));label("$Q$",Q,N);label("$y=mx$",(9.2,9.2*m),E,red);
label("$x$",(10,0),S);label("$y$",(0,9),W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Points of $AB$ are $A+t(B-A)=(2t,8-8t)$ and points of $AC$ are $A+u(C-A)=(8u,8-8u)$. Solving with $y=mx$ gives
\[
t=\dfrac{4}{4+m}\ \ \Bigl(P=\Bigl(\dfrac{8}{4+m},\dfrac{8m}{4+m}\Bigr)\Bigr),\qquad u=\dfrac{1}{1+m}\ \ \Bigl(Q=\Bigl(\dfrac{8}{1+m},\dfrac{8m}{1+m}\Bigr)\Bigr).
\]
Triangle $APQ$ shares the angle at $A$ with triangle $ABC$, so $[APQ]=tu\,[ABC]$. We need $tu=\dfrac{1}{2}$:
\[
\dfrac{4}{(4+m)(1+m)}=\dfrac{1}{2}\iff m^2+5m+4=8\iff m^2+5m-4=0,
\]
so $m=\dfrac{-5+\sqrt{41}}{2}\approx0.70$ (the other root is negative). Then $t\approx0.85$ and $u\approx0.59$ lie in $(0,1)$, as required. The slope plus the $y$-intercept is
\[
\dfrac{-5+\sqrt{41}}{2}+0=\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt{41}-5}{2}}.
\]

question:
Square $ABCD$ has side length $1$. Four quarter circles of radius $1$ are drawn inside the square, centred at $A$, $B$, $C$ and $D$. What is the area of the region that lies inside all four quarter circles (shaded)?

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(1,0),C=(1,1),D=(0,1);
real h=sqrt(3)/2;
pair P1=(0.5,1-h),P2=(h,0.5),P3=(0.5,h),P4=(1-h,0.5);
