question:
Let $A(x_1,y_1)$ and $B(x_2,y_2)$ be distinct lattice points (points with integer coordinates).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that the number of lattice points on segment $AB$, including $A$ and $B$, is $\gcd\left(|x_2-x_1|,|y_2-y_1|\right)+1$ (where $\gcd(0,k)=k$).
\item[(b)] How many lattice points lie on the segment from $(3,-2)$ to $(15,7)$, including the endpoints?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
(a) Let $a=x_2-x_1$, $b=y_2-y_1$ (not both $0$) and $g=\gcd(|a|,|b|)\ge1$. The points of the segment are $X(t)=(x_1+ta,\ y_1+tb)$ for $0\le t\le1$, and $X(t)$ is a lattice point exactly when $ta$ and $tb$ are both integers.

If $t=\dfrac {k}{g}$ with $k$ an integer, then $ta=k\cdot\dfrac {a}{g}$ and $tb=k\cdot\dfrac {b}{g}$ are integers. Conversely, by B\'ezout there are integers $\alpha,\beta$ with $\alpha a+\beta b=g$; if $ta$ and $tb$ are integers, then $tg=\alpha(ta)+\beta(tb)$ is an integer, so $t=\dfrac {k}{g}$ for some integer $k$. Hence the lattice points correspond to $t=0,\dfrac{1}{g},\dfrac{2}{g},\dots,1$, and there are exactly $g+1$ of them.

(b) Here $a=12$, $b=9$ and $g=\gcd(12,9)=3$, so there are $\boxed{4}$ lattice points: $(3,-2)$, $(7,1)$, $(11,4)$, $(15,7)$, spaced by $\left(\dfrac{12}{3},\dfrac{9}{3}\right)=(4,3)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=3;i<=15;++i) draw((i,-2)--(i,7),gray(0.8)+linewidth(0.3));
for(int j=-2;j<=7;++j) draw((3,j)--(15,j),gray(0.8)+linewidth(0.3));
pair A=(3,-2),B=(15,7);
draw(A--B,linewidth(0.9));
for(int k=0;k<=3;++k) dot(A+k*(4,3),red);
label("$A(3,-2)$",A,SE);label("$B(15,7)$",B,NW);
label("$(7,1)$",A+(4,3),SE,fontsize(8pt));label("$(11,4)$",A+(8,6),SE,fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the polynomial $x^3+35x^2-50x+24$ has no rational roots, and deduce that it cannot be factored into polynomials of lower degree with integer coefficients.
\item[(b)] Expand $(x+2)(x-3)(x+5)(x+6)$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Put the centre at $O=(0,0)$ and the corners at $(\pm1,\pm1)$. A point $(x,y)$ of the board is closer to $O$ than to the corner $(1,1)$ exactly when $x^2+y^2<(x-1)^2+(y-1)^2$, i.e.\ $x+y<1$; similarly for the other corners, so "closer to the centre than to every corner" is the inside of the dashed square $|x|+|y|<1$.

A point within distance $1$ of the corner $(1,1)$ that also satisfies $x+y<1$ lies in the circular segment cut from the quarter disc of radius $1$ about $(1,1)$ by the chord from $(1,0)$ to $(0,1)$ (both endpoints are on the circle). Such points have $x,y\ge0$ and are automatically on the correct side of the other three lines.

