The answer is: \textbf{infinitely many}. In fact every point of the parabola is a vertex of such a triangle.

Since $y=(x+1)^2+3$, translating by $(1,-3)$ (which preserves distances) turns the graph into the parabola $\mathcal P:\ y=x^2$, so we work with $\mathcal P$. Fix $A=(a,a^2)$ on $\mathcal P$ and let $\rho$ be the rotation about $A$ through $60^\circ$ counterclockwise, $\rho(A+(p,q))=A+\left(\dfrac {p}{2}-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}q,\ \dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}p+\dfrac {q}{2}\right)$. For a point $B\ne A$ on $\mathcal P$, the triangle $A,B,\rho(B)$ is equilateral; we need $\rho(B)$ to lie on $\mathcal P$ as well.

Put $B=(t,t^2)$ and $\rho(B)=(u(t),v(t))$:
\[
u(t)=a+\dfrac{t-a}{2}-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}(t^2-a^2),\qquad v(t)=a^2+\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}(t-a)+\dfrac{t^2-a^2}{2},
\]
and let $g(t)=v(t)-u(t)^2$, so $\rho(B)\in\mathcal P$ exactly when $g(t)=0$. Then:
\begin{itemize}
\item $g(a)=a^2-a^2=0$, and $g'(a)=v'(a)-2u(a)u'(a)=\left(\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}+a\right)-2a\left(\dfrac{1}{2}-\sqrt3a\right)=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}+2\sqrt3a^2>0$, so $g(t)>0$ for $t$ slightly larger than $a$.
\item As $t\to\infty$, $u(t)^2$ grows like $\dfrac{3}{4}t^4$ while $v(t)$ grows like $\dfrac{1}{2}t^2$, so $g(t)\to-\infty$.
\end{itemize}
By the intermediate value theorem $g(b)=0$ for some $b>a$. With $B=(b,b^2)$ and $C=\rho(B)$, both on $\mathcal P$ and distinct from $A$, the triangle $ABC$ is equilateral.

So every point $A$ of the parabola is a vertex of an inscribed equilateral triangle. Each triangle has only three vertices and there are infinitely many points $A$, hence infinitely many such triangles. Two of them are drawn: the one symmetric about the axis, with vertices $(-1,3)$ and $(-1\pm\sqrt3,6)$ (side $2\sqrt3$), and the one with a vertex at $(0,4)$.

