question:
A $2\times2$ matrix $\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{pmatrix}$ is generated by choosing each of the four entries independently and uniformly from the integers $0,1,\dots,25$. For the Hill cipher the matrix must be invertible modulo $26$, which happens if and only if $ad-bc$ is relatively prime to $26$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What is the probability that the matrix is \emph{not} invertible?
\item[(b)] More generally, an $n\times n$ matrix with entries chosen the same way is invertible if its determinant is relatively prime to $26$. Find the probability that it is not invertible.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
An angle inscribed in a semicircle is a right angle, so $\angle ABC=90^\circ$, and $AC=2\cdot13=26$.

