There are $4^4=256$ equally likely assignments of the chips. Count the complement, where some cookie gets at least $3$ chips (only one cookie can):
\begin{itemize}
\item exactly $3$ chips in one cookie: $4$ choices of cookie, $\dbinom43=4$ choices of chips, and $3$ cookies for the last chip: $48$;
\item all $4$ chips in one cookie: $4$.
\end{itemize}
So $52$ assignments are bad, and the probability is $\dfrac{256-52}{256}=\dfrac{204}{256}=\boxed{\dfrac{51}{64}}$.

question:
A store is having a sale on soap: for every three bars of soap purchased, one more bar is given free. Each bar costs $\$2$, and there is a limit of eight bars per customer (free bars included). A customer takes home $b$ bars and pays as little as possible.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Write a piecewise-defined function $C(b)$ for the total cost in dollars.
\item State the domain of $C$ in the context of the problem.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Triangles $AEF$ and $ABC$ share the angle at $A$, so their areas are in the ratio of the products of the sides enclosing that angle:
\[
\dfrac{[AEF]}{[ABC]}=\dfrac{\dfrac{1}{2}\,AE\cdot AF\sin A}{\dfrac{1}{2}\,AB\cdot AC\sin A}=\dfrac{AE}{AB}\cdot\dfrac{AF}{AC}=\dfrac{1}{3}\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}=\dfrac{1}{6} .
\]
(Without trigonometry: $[AEC]=\dfrac{1}{3}[ABC]$ because the two triangles have the same height from $C$ and bases $AE=\dfrac{1}{3}AB$; and $[AEF]=\dfrac{1}{2}[AEC]$ because $F$ is the midpoint of $AC$.)

\begin{asy}
