Triangle $ABC$ has base $BC=6$ and height $8$, so area $24$. Areas scale by $k^2$: $24k^2=13.5$ gives $k^2=\dfrac{9}{16}$, $k=\dfrac{3}{4}$, so $8-t=6$ and $t=\boxed{2}$. (Then $T=(1.5,2)$, $U=(6,2)$, $TU=4.5$, and $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot4.5\cdot6=13.5$.)

The moves have lengths $1,2,\dots,m$ with total $1+2+\dots+m=28$, so $m=7$. A path is determined by the set of move lengths that go in the $x$-direction, which must sum to $14$ (the rest then also sum to $14$). Count subsets of $\{1,\dots,7\}$ with sum $14$, sorted by size:
\begin{itemize}
\item three elements: $\{7,6,1\},\{7,5,2\},\{7,4,3\},\{6,5,3\}$;
\item four elements: their complements $\{2,3,4,5\},\{1,3,4,6\},\{1,2,5,6\},\{1,2,4,7\}$;
\item two or fewer elements give at most $7+6=13$, and five or more are complements of those.
\end{itemize}
So there are $\boxed{8}$ paths.

The condition $p_i\le\dfrac{p_{i-1}+p_{i+1}}{2}$ says $p_i-p_{i-1}\le p_{i+1}-p_i$: the successive differences never decrease. As the terms are distinct, no difference is $0$, so the sequence decreases down to its smallest term $1$ and then increases. Reading outward from $1$ on either side, the terms increase and the gaps between neighbours never decrease. The two sides are otherwise independent (at $1$ the condition holds automatically, a negative difference being followed by a positive one).

The number $2$ is next to $1$ (it is the nearest term on its side). By reversing the sequence we may assume $2$ is to the right of $1$, and we double at the end. Let the right side start $1,2,\dots,k$ with gaps $1$ and let $k$ be maximal ($2\le k\le8$).
\begin{itemize}
\item $k=8$: the sequence $1,2,\dots,8$.
\item $k=7$: then $8$ is on the left: $8,1,2,\dots,7$.
\item $k\le6$: then $k+1$ is the first term on the left, with gap $k$ from $1$, so every later left gap is at least $k$; and every further right gap is at least $2$.
 \begin{itemize}
 \item If $k\ge3$: the number $k+2\le8$ cannot be on the left (the next left term is at least $2k+1>k+2$), so it is on the right with gap $2$. If $k\le5$, then $k+3\le8$ fits neither on the right (gap $1<2$) nor on the left (it is less than $2k+1$). So $k=6$, giving $7,1,2,3,4,5,6,8$.
 \item If $k=2$: the left side starts $1,3$ with gaps at least $2$, the right side $1,2$ continues with gaps at least $2$. Then $4$ must go right, so $5$ must go left, so $6$ goes right, $7$ left, $8$ right, every gap being $2$: the sequence $7,5,3,1,2,4,6,8$.
 \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}
That is $4$ sequences with $2$ to the right of $1$, and $4$ mirror images, $\boxed{8}$ in all.

