Let $AB=w$ and $BC=h$. Then $BE=\dfrac {h}{3}$ and $EC=\dfrac{2h}{3}$, and $FC=\dfrac {w}{2}$. Triangles $EBG$ and $ECF$ are similar (vertical angles at $E$ and right angles at $B$ and $C$), so
\[
\dfrac{BG}{CF}=\dfrac{BE}{EC}=\dfrac{1}{2},\qquad BG=\dfrac {w}{4},\qquad AG=w+\dfrac {w}{4}=\dfrac{5w}{4}.
\]
Now $AG\parallel CF$, so triangles $APG$ and $CPF$ are similar (alternate angles), and
\[
\dfrac{AP}{PC}=\dfrac{AG}{CF}=\dfrac{5w}{4}\cdot\dfrac{2}{w}=\boxed{\dfrac{5}{2}}.
\]
(Check with coordinates $A=(0,0)$, $C=(w,h)$, $E=\left(w,\dfrac {h}{3}\right)$, $F=\left(\dfrac {w}{2},h\right)$: the lines meet at $P=\dfrac{5}{7}\,C$.)

There are $36$ equally likely ordered outcomes. Count the products greater than $10$ by the first die:
\begin{itemize}
\item first die $1$: none; first die $2$: second die $6$ ($1$ outcome);
\item first die $3$: $4,5,6$ ($3$); first die $4$: $3,4,5,6$ ($4$);
\item first die $5$: $3,4,5,6$ ($4$); first die $6$: $2,3,4,5,6$ ($5$).
\end{itemize}
That is $1+3+4+4+5=17$ outcomes, so the probability is $\dfrac{17}{36}$, answer $\boxed{\textbf{(B)}}$.

By the law of cosines,
\[
\cos A=\dfrac{AB^2+AC^2-BC^2}{2\cdot AB\cdot AC}=\dfrac{196+169-225}{364}=\dfrac{5}{13},\qquad \sin A=\dfrac{12}{13}.
\]
Angle $A$ is acute, so the circumcentre $X$ lies on the same side of $BC$ as $A$, and the central angle is $\angle BXC=2\angle A$:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
