\[
V=\dfrac{1}{3}\pi r^2h=\dfrac{1}{3}\pi\cdot5^2\cdot3=25\pi\approx\boxed{78.54}\text{ cm}^3 .
\]

question:
The actual attendance at a game in Atlanta is $100{,}000$, and Anita made an estimate $E$ of it. Find all values of $E$ for which each statement is true:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] the actual attendance is within $10\%$ of Anita's estimate;
\item[(ii)] Anita's estimate is within $10\%$ of the actual attendance.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
A point $P=(x,y)$ is at distance $\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$ from the origin $O$ and at distance $\dfrac{|3x-2y-6|}{\sqrt{3^2+2^2}}=\dfrac{|3x-2y-6|}{\sqrt{13}}$ from the line. Setting these equal and squaring (both sides are nonnegative),
\[
13(x^2+y^2)=(3x-2y-6)^2=9x^2+4y^2+36-12xy-36x+24y ,
\]
which simplifies to
\[
\boxed{4x^2+12xy+9y^2+36x-24y-36=0}.
\]
This is the parabola with focus $O$ and directrix $3x-2y-6=0$ (note $4x^2+12xy+9y^2=(2x+3y)^2$, a perfect square, as for every parabola):

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real p=3/sqrt(13); pair nn=(3,-2)/sqrt(13), t=(2,3)/sqrt(13), V=(9/13,-6/13);
guide g; for(int i=0;i<=80;++i){real v=-4.6+9.2*i/80; g=g..(V-(v^2/(4p))*nn+v*t);}
draw((-6.5,0)--(4.5,0),gray(0.5),Arrow(TeXHead));draw((0,-4.5)--(0,6),gray(0.5),Arrow(TeXHead));
label("$x$",(4.5,0),S,gray(0.3));label("$y$",(0,6),W,gray(0.3));
draw((-0.6,-3.9)--(3.6,2.4),red+linewidth(0.9));
label("$3x-2y-6=0$",(3.6,2.4),E,red);
draw(g,blue+linewidth(0.9));
real v0=-3; pair P=V-(v0^2/(4p))*nn+v0*t; pair Fp=P-((3*P.x-2*P.y-6)/13)*(3,-2);
draw(P--(0,0),dashed);draw(P--Fp,dashed);
pair u=unit(P-Fp),w=unit((2,3));
draw(Fp+0.25*u--Fp+0.25*u+0.25*w--Fp+0.25*w);
dot((0,0));dot(P);
label("$O$",(0,0),dir(135));label("$P$",P,dir(235));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Let the circumradius be $R$ and the centre $O$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=7; real al=2*aSin(1/7), be=60, ga=2*aSin(11/14);
real[] arcs={al,al,be,be,ga,ga};
pair[] P; real t=100; for(int i=0;i<6;++i){P.push(R*dir(t)); t-=arcs[i];}
draw(circle((0,0),R),gray(0.6));
draw(P[0]--P[1]--P[2]--P[3]--P[4]--P[5]--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
string[] lab={"$2$","$2$","$7$","$7$","$11$","$11$"};
for(int i=0;i<6;++i){pair m=(P[i]+P[(i+1)%6])/2; label(lab[i],(R+0.75)*unit(m));}
dot((0,0));label("$O$",(0,0),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

A chord of length $\ell$ cuts off an arc whose central angle depends only on $\ell$ and $R$. So reordering the sides around the circle keeps the same circle: the hexagon with sides $2,7,11,2,7,11$ in this order also has circumradius $R$. In that hexagon the arcs of the sides $2,7,11$ make up half of the circle, so the vertices $P_0$ and $P_3$ at the ends of the path $2,7,11$ are diametrically opposite. Thus $P_0P_1P_2P_3$ is a cyclic quadrilateral with sides $a=2$, $b=7$, $c=11$ and the diameter $d=P_0P_3=2R$:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=7; real x=aSin(1/7), y=30, z=aSin(11/14);
pair P0=R*dir(180),P1=R*dir(180-2*x),P2=R*dir(180-2*x-2*y),P3=R*dir(0);
draw(arc((0,0),R,0,180),gray(0.6));
draw(P0--P1--P2--P3--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(P0--P2,dashed);draw(P1--P3,dashed);
label("$2$",(P0+P1)/2,W);label("$7$",(R+0.75)*unit((P1+P2)/2));label("$11$",(R+0.75)*unit((P2+P3)/2));
label("$d=2R$",(0,0),S);
label("$P_0$",P0,SW);label("$P_1$",P1,NW);label("$P_2$",P2,N);label("$P_3$",P3,E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The angles at $P_1$ and $P_2$ are inscribed in a semicircle, so they are right angles, and the diagonals are $P_0P_2=\sqrt{d^2-c^2}$ and $P_1P_3=\sqrt{d^2-a^2}$. Ptolemy's theorem gives $P_0P_2\cdot P_1P_3=ac+bd$, so
\[
(d^2-a^2)(d^2-c^2)=(ac+bd)^2\ \Longrightarrow\ d^4-(a^2+c^2)d^2=2abcd+b^2d^2\ \Longrightarrow\ d^3-(a^2+b^2+c^2)d-2abc=0 .
\]
With $a=2,b=7,c=11$: $d^3-174d-308=0$, which factors as $(d-14)(d^2+14d+22)=0$. The quadratic factor has only negative roots, so $d=14$ and the circumradius is $R=\boxed{7}$.

(Check: the half-angles $\arcsin\dfrac{1}{7}$, $\arcsin\dfrac{1}{2}=30^\circ$ and $\arcsin\dfrac{11}{14}$ add up to $90^\circ$, since $\cos\left(\arcsin\dfrac{1}{7}+30^\circ\right)=\dfrac{4\sqrt3}{7}\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}-\dfrac{1}{7}\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}=\dfrac{11}{14}$.)

The bases add up to $23$ and differ by $17$, so they are $20$ and $3$; the legs add up to $23$ and differ by $7$, so they are $15$ and $8$. Let $ABCD$ have bases $AB=20$, $DC=3$ and legs $AD=8$, $BC=15$. Through $C$ draw $CE\parallel DA$ with $E$ on $AB$. Then $AECD$ is a parallelogram, $AE=3$, $CE=8$ and $EB=17$:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real h=120/17,p=64/17; pair A=(0,0),B=(20,0),D=(p,h),C=(p+3,h),Ep=(3,0);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(C--Ep,dashed+blue);
pair u=unit(Ep-C),w=unit(B-C);draw(C+0.7*u--C+0.7*u+0.7*w--C+0.7*w,blue);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$D$",D,N);label("$E$",Ep,S);
label("$8$",(A+D)/2,W);label("$15$",(B+C)/2,NE);label("$3$",(C+D)/2,N);
label("$3$",(A+Ep)/2,S);label("$17$",(Ep+B)/2,S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Triangle $ECB$ has sides $8,15,17$, and $8^2+15^2=17^2$, so it has a right angle at $C$. Its height to $EB$, which is the height of the trapezoid, is
\[
h=\dfrac{8\cdot15}{17}=\dfrac{120}{17}.
\]
The area is $\dfrac{20+3}{2}\cdot\dfrac{120}{17}=\dfrac{1380}{17}$, a reduced fraction, so $x-y=1380-17=\boxed{1363}$.

By symmetry the foot of the altitude from $D$ is the centre $O$ of the equilateral triangle $ABC$, and the centre $I$ of the inscribed sphere lies on $DO$ with $IO=11$. Let $M$ be the midpoint of $BC$ and $r_0=OM$ the inradius of triangle $ABC$. The plane $DOM$ is perpendicular to the face $DBC$ (it contains $DM$ and is perpendicular to $BC$), so in this plane the inscribed sphere appears as a circle of radius $11$ tangent to $OM$ at $O$ and to $DM$ at a point $T$ (drawn to scale):

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r0=22*sqrt(3),h=24,r=11; pair O=(0,0),M=(r0,0),D=(0,h),I=(0,r);
pair T=D+dot(I-D,unit(M-D))*unit(M-D);
draw(O--M--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(arc(I,r,-90,90),blue);
draw(I--T,blue+dashed);
draw(O+(1.4,0)--O+(1.4,1.4)--O+(0,1.4));
dot(I);
label("$D$",D,N);label("$O$",O,SW);label("$M$",M,SE);label("$I$",I,W);label("$T$",T,NE);
label("$11$",(I+T)/2,dir(-45),blue);
draw((-5,0)--(-5,h),Arrows(TeXHead));label("$24$",(-5,h/2),W);
label("$r_0$",(r0/2,0),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Let $\varphi=\angle DMO$. Since $I$ is equidistant from the lines $MO$ and $MD$, $MI$ bisects $\varphi$, so
\[
\tan\dfrac{\varphi}{2}=\dfrac{11}{r_0},\qquad \tan\varphi=\dfrac{24}{r_0}.
\]
By the double-angle formula, $\dfrac{22}{r_0}\div\left(1-\dfrac{121}{r_0^2}\right)=\dfrac{24}{r_0}$, so $22=24\left(1-\dfrac{121}{r_0^2}\right)$, which gives $\dfrac{121}{r_0^2}=\dfrac{1}{12}$ and $r_0^2=1452$, $r_0=22\sqrt3$. The inradius of an equilateral triangle with side $s$ is $\dfrac{s}{2\sqrt3}$, so
\[
AB=2\sqrt3\,r_0=2\sqrt3\cdot22\sqrt3=\boxed{132}.
\]
(Check with $r=\dfrac{3V}{\text{surface area}}$: the slant height is $DM=\sqrt{24^2+1452}=\sqrt{2028}=26\sqrt3$, the surface area is $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\cdot132^2+3\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot132\cdot26\sqrt3=4356\sqrt3+5148\sqrt3=9504\sqrt3$, and $V=\dfrac{1}{3}\cdot4356\sqrt3\cdot24=34848\sqrt3$, so $r=\dfrac{3\cdot34848}{9504}=11$.)

Let the right angle be at $C$, with legs $CB=a$ and $CA=b$, and let $M$ and $K$ be the midpoints of $CB$ and $CA$:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0),B=(12,0),A=(0,8),M=(6,0),K=(0,4);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--M,blue+dashed);draw(B--K,blue+dashed);
draw((0,0.7)--(0.7,0.7)--(0.7,0));
draw((3,-0.25)--(3,0.25));draw((9,-0.25)--(9,0.25));
draw((-0.25,2)--(0.25,2));draw((-0.25,6)--(0.25,6));
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,E);label("$C$",C,SW);label("$M$",M,S);label("$K$",K,W);
label("$10$",(3.9,4.6),blue);label("$4\sqrt{10}$",(6.6,2.5),blue+fontsize(9pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

By Pythagoras in the right triangles $ACM$ and $BCK$,
\[
AM^2=b^2+\dfrac{a^2}{4}=100,\qquad BK^2=a^2+\dfrac{b^2}{4}=160 .
\]
Adding, $\dfrac{5}{4}(a^2+b^2)=260$, so $AB^2=a^2+b^2=208$ and $AB=\sqrt{208}=\boxed{4\sqrt{13}}$. (The legs are $a=12$ and $b=8$.)

We need $x>1$. The hypotenuse is the longest side, and $x-1<5x$ and $x-1<4x+13$, so it is $5x$ or $4x+13$.

If the hypotenuse is $5x$: $25x^2=(4x+13)^2+(x-1)^2$ gives $8x^2-102x-170=0$, i.e.\ $4x^2-51x-85=0$, whose discriminant $51^2+4\cdot4\cdot85=3961$ is not a perfect square ($62^2<3961<63^2$), so there is no integer root.

If the hypotenuse is $4x+13$: $(4x+13)^2=25x^2+(x-1)^2$ gives $10x^2-106x-168=0$, i.e.\ $5x^2-53x-84=0=(x-12)(5x+7)$. So $x=12$, and the sides are $60$, $61$, $11$; indeed $11^2+60^2=121+3600=3721=61^2$:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0),B=(60,0),A=(0,11);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));draw((0,2.2)--(2.2,2.2)--(2.2,0));
label("$5x=60$",(30,0),S);label("$x-1=11$",(0,5.5),W);label("$4x+13=61$",(30,5.5),NE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The sum of the digits of $x=12$ is $\boxed{3}$.

By the law of cosines, $\cos B=\dfrac{25+256-153}{2\cdot5\cdot16}=\dfrac{128}{160}=\dfrac{4}{5}$, so the foot $H$ of the altitude from $A$ satisfies $BH=4$, and $AH=3$; then $HC=12$ and indeed $AC^2=3^2+12^2=153$:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(16,0),A=(4,3),H=(4,0),D=(11,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--H,dashed);draw(A--D,blue);
draw((4,0)--(4.5,0)--(4.5,0.5)--(4,0.5));
dot(D,blue);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,W);label("$C$",C,E);label("$H$",H,S);label("$D$",D,S,blue);
label("$5$",(A+B)/2,NW);label("$\sqrt{153}$",(A+C)/2,NE);label("$3$",(4,1.5),W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

As $D$ moves along $BC$, $AD=\sqrt{9+HD^2}$ takes every value from its minimum $AH=3$ (at $D=H$) up to its maximum $AC=\sqrt{153}\approx12.37$ (at $D=C$), continuously. So the possible integer values are $3,4,\dots,12$, with sum $\dfrac{(3+12)\cdot10}{2}=\boxed{75}$.

For a common tangent segment $T_1T_2$, the radii $OT_1$ and $AT_2$ are both perpendicular to it:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0),A=(26,0); real r=5,R=13;
pair n1=(-8/26,sqrt(612)/26), n2=(18/26,-sqrt(352)/26);
pair T1=O+r*n1,T2=A+R*n1,U1=O+r*n2,U2=A-R*n2;
draw(circle(O,r),linewidth(0.8));draw(circle(A,R),linewidth(0.8));
draw(O--A,dashed);
draw(T1--T2,blue+linewidth(0.9));draw(U1--U2,red+linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--T1,gray(0.5));draw(A--T2,gray(0.5));draw(O--U1,gray(0.5));draw(A--U2,gray(0.5));
dot(O);dot(A);
label("$O$",O,W);label("$A$",A,E);label("$26$",(19,0),S);
label("$6\sqrt{17}$",(T1+T2)/2,dir(100),blue);label("$4\sqrt{22}$",(8.6,-3.0),red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

For an external tangent the radii point to the same side, and sliding $T_1T_2$ along the radii gives a right triangle with hypotenuse $OA=26$, one leg $13-5=8$ and the other leg equal to the tangent length:
\[
\sqrt{26^2-8^2}=\sqrt{612}=6\sqrt{17}.
\]
For an internal tangent the radii point to opposite sides, and the leg is $13+5=18$:
\[
\sqrt{26^2-18^2}=\sqrt{352}=4\sqrt{22}.
\]
The sum is $\boxed{6\sqrt{17}+4\sqrt{22}}$.

Since $\angle ABC=90^\circ$, $AC$ is a diameter and $X$ is its midpoint. Here $AC=\sqrt{80^2+18^2}=\sqrt{6724}=82$, so $AX=41$:

